## I. The Fall of Lucifer, and the Life of Adam and Eve

### folios 004v–006v

The book does not begin with a gospel. It begins before the world, and it
begins by telling you who is being told.

In the beginning the Lord God was, and the heart of heaven and earth, and the
sun and the moon were written (004v). Then a voice enters that will not leave
the book for four hundred leaves: *the angel of God says to Elijah the
prophet.* Everything that follows is addressed to Elijah, delivered in
numbered sayings, and the numbers run on across the pages — *and six, says
God's angel to Elijah the prophet* (001r), *and seven, says God's angel to
Elijah the prophet* (001r). Whatever this manuscript is, it presents itself as
a revelation given to one man in order.

What the angel tells him first is the fall. Two hundred and fifty-seven years
before Adam, the angels were made, angels upon angels (004v). One of them has
a name the script writes as a single sign — the sign for *hiding oneself*
joined to the sign for *angel* — and he prays forty days and forty nights
(004v), and he is proud (004r). He envies. He sets himself on the throne,
equal to the Lord's Lord (002r).

Between those two pages stands one of the strangest leaves in the codex.
Folio 004r says almost nothing but one thing, over and over, in slightly
different order each time: an angel above on the throne, and the cup, and the
hiding angel, and *he stole the cup, and hid himself above, and the two went*.
The page circles. It is not clear what the cup is, and this edition does not
pretend to know; the lines are printed at the back exactly as they stand, and
they are worth looking at, because a page that repeats itself twelve times is
either liturgy or damage, and it does not look like damage.

Then the episode that identifies the whole book. God commands the angels to
bow. Michael the angel, the believing servant, stands up (002r). The command
goes out from the eternal Father, and every angel bows — and the hiding angel
*refused to go from the Father* (002r). That scene is not in Genesis. It is
the distinguishing episode of the apocryphal *Life of Adam and Eve*, and its
presence here, in the second leaf of the manuscript, is what tells you what
the compiler was working from.

The making of man follows, and the codex takes it slowly. The Father and the
Son and the Holy Spirit are not many but one God (002v) — a Trinity formula,
stated flatly, before anything is made. Then: *the Lord, man, the heart, the
slime of the earth, and then man existed … and became to soul one. The heart
breathed on Adam, and living left, and man grabbed the Lord, and man went
inside the Garden of Eden* (002v). That is Genesis 2:7 — the dust, the breath,
the living being — in the manuscript's own compressed grammar.

Adam in the garden is righteous, and *nor was he hungry or thirsty, and he did
not have sin* (003r). He is given one law, one yoke, one commandment: *do not
eat* (003r). Then the sleep, and the rib, and Eve — and the codex marks the
moment with a line of its own devising: *bone bone, in turn two souls, one*
(003v).

The serpent is on the tree that stood in the midst (003v), and the argument on
that page runs the way it runs in every telling: not *shall not eat* but *eat*,
because Adam, Master, through commandment. Then the fruit, and Adam ate, and
Adam knew (003v). *Evil and good, how does the Lord God know?* (001r). Their
eyes are opened upon the two of them, naked; they see it; they are ashamed
(001r).

The interrogation on 001v is one of the places where the manuscript's English
comes out nearly clean. The Lord goes into Paradise and says, *Adam, why?* And
Adam hides himself from the Lord. And the Lord says, *Who told you to hide
yourself?* Eve is asked, and answers, and the serpent gave it food. Note what
the scribe does here, and he does it more than once in this book: Adam, caught,
*hides himself* — the same sign, the same word, that names Lucifer.

The sentence follows: pain, and the ground cursed, and Adam on the earth
tilling the earth and wanting food (007r). The Lord's angel, and a flaming
sword, and the gate (007r); a cherub set on the gate of the Garden of Eden
(007v).

And then the codex leaves Genesis and goes into the legend that will carry the
rest of the book. Outside the garden Adam and Eve have three sons — *the
firstborn is Cain, and the second is Abel, and the third is Seth* — and Adam
grows old and goes blind (007v). Blind, he sends his son: *carry Adam from the
tree of mercy* (007v). Seth goes to the gate of Paradise and an angel answers
him (006r), and what he asks for is the oil of mercy, the anointing that gives
light, *through the eye, blind Adam sees* (006r). The angel sends him back
with a branch from the tree (006r, 006v).

That branch is the seed of everything this manuscript cares about. It is
planted, and it grows, and four hundred leaves later an emperor will take off
his purple and his shoes and carry what was made of it back into Jerusalem on
his bare feet. The compiler knew that when he wrote page six.

