## X. The Last Things, and the Writer's Own Days

### folios 221r–224v

The last eight leaves of the codex stop being a book about the past. They
become a book about the man holding the pen.

They begin with a table. Not a narrative — a list, by weekday, of what the
sky has done and what it means. *On Friday the earth quaked. On Wednesday the
moon was eclipsed for one hour* (221v). *Three days before the feast of the
Virgin Mary, on Sunday, the earth quaked* (221v). *A sign shall appear in
heaven … the sun darkened within the holy church* (221v). *A wind shall come
from the spirit, the earth shall quake, and the moon* (223r). Between the
portents the compiler keeps putting the same two sentences, the ones that run
through the whole manuscript like a drone: *whosoever believes and carries the
Lord shall be saved; whosoever believes not shall perish* (223r).

Then, on 223v, the book says when it is.

It does not say 1593, and for a while that looked like a quarrel with the
published date. It is not. Read the first line before the number: *from the
departure of the Lord Jesus Christ to the Father* (223v, line 1) — the count
is from the Ascension, not from the Nativity. The number that follows is *a
thousand years, five hundred, and sixty* (223v, line 2). Put the Ascension at
its traditional AD 33 and the sum is **1593**, which is the year Kiraly and
Tokai read in this manuscript by their own route. The codex and the scholars
agree; they were simply counting from different events, and the codex says
which one it is counting from on the line above the number.

The same page carries a second number, and it is the sharpest external anchor
in the book. The age of the world at the Nativity is given as *five thousand,
and a hundred, and nine-ten, and nine* (223v, lines 9–10) — 5,199. That is
precisely the figure of the Roman Martyrology's Christmas proclamation, the
number a sixteenth-century Catholic heard read aloud every Christmas Eve. It
is not a number anybody could arrive at by guessing, and nothing about the
method that produced this edition could have invented it.

Two cautions belong here, in the text and not in a footnote. First, both of
those numbers had *also* been restored, separately, as single signs elsewhere
on this same page — guessed from the note saying what the page was expected to
contain. Those two guesses have been withdrawn and now read *a numeral*,
because a guess that manufactures the anchor it was supposed to test is worth
nothing. Both numbers survive without them, spelled out sign by sign in
readings that stand on their own. Second, the manuscript's arithmetic is not
tidy even where it is legible: the word *day* is attached to the hundred in
line 9, and line 6 reads *the son of Moses* where the sense wants something
else. This is a real page of a real book, not a clean one.

The numerals themselves are checkable three ways, and all three are in the
back of this volume. The codex writes the Twelve as **six-six** — that phrase
occurs on thirteen lines and nine of them name the apostles. It writes *six
hundred and six-ten and six* twice, at 028v and 159r, and both times the
words beside it are *hell* and *evil*: the number of the beast, Revelation
13:18. And it spells 5,199 out in pieces that each carry their own weight.

After the date the disciples ask the obvious question — *Master, when shall
the judgment day be?* (223v) — and 222r answers it in the manner of the genre:
*after two thousand years* … *the man of sin dies, the man of sin is damned,
and is saved* … *the earth, the water, the sun, all shall be shaken* (222r).
That last formula, the one about being saved and damned, comes out of the
gloss with its polarity scrambled from folio to folio, and this edition has
not straightened it. It is almost certainly a fixed creedal tag whose word
order the rendering is not capturing. It is named as an open problem in the
apparatus rather than quietly normalised here.

And then, on 222v, the manuscript does the thing that no amount of
decipherment prepares you for. It stops preaching and starts keeping a diary.

Six times on that page stands the sign Kiraly and Tokai gloss as *the name of
the author*. It is the only page in four hundred and forty-one where the man
who made the book is the subject of it. What he writes down is where he went
and what he did:

> *On the holy day, Monday, I went to the house.*
> *The brother carried me to the Lord's house on the Friday.*
> *And then I went to the Lord's house on the Sunday.*
> *In holy Philip's year, on the feast, Monday.*
> *I pray — my sins — Lord, have mercy.*
> *On the Friday I fasted.*

Two more leaves follow, 224r and 224v, and they are the worst-preserved in the
codex: Michael and Matthew and Saturdays, a portion of wine, *and one and a
half three, and one and a half three* (224r). They cite no source, and almost
every guess on them is a placeholder; the apparatus says so. The final line
of the book asks that a soul go up into heaven, *from loss, from riches at the
last, exists for ever, amen* (224v).

But the ending that matters is the one on 222v. Whatever else this book is —
compilation, cipher, sermon-cycle, two hundred years of scholarly
embarrassment — a man sat down in 1593, in a script he had built so that only
he could read it, and wrote: *on the Friday I fasted. I pray. My sins. Lord,
have mercy.*

